   $q$Three proportional relationships are graphed on the same axes. Line P passes through the origin and $(4, 10)$. Line Q is the graph of $y = 2.2x$. Line R comes from a table where $x = 5$ gives $y = 12$. Which line is steepest?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Line P","is_correct":true},{"text":"Line Q","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"compared-inverted-rates"},{"text":"All three lines are equally steep","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"assumed-proportional-lines-share-slope"},{"text":"Line R","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"compared-y-values"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Line P$q$, $q$Find each slope: Line P is $10 \div 4 = 2.5$, Line Q is $2.2$, and Line R is $12 \div 5 = 2.4$. The greatest slope is $2.5$, so Line P is steepest. Choosing Line R because 12 is the biggest $y$-value ignores that its $x$-value is also bigger.$q$, 'draft', null)
